## Why This Problem Is Asked

This problem explores non-overlapping palindromic substring selection using a two-dimensional DP that tracks prefix length and the number of selected substrings. It tests palindrome precomputation, transition logic, and solution reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Palindrome table**: pal[l][r] indicates whether s[l..r] is a palindrome
- **DP state**: dp[i][t] represents the maximum total length obtainable from prefix s[0..i] using exactly t substrings
- **Transition**: Either skip the current position or end a palindrome at i (starting at some l)
- **Reconstruction**: Use parent pointers to backtrack and retrieve the selected substrings

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalindromes(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pal[i][i] = true;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len - 1 < n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      if (s[l] === s[r] && (len === 2 || pal[l+1][r-1])) {
        pal[l][r] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1]?.[t] ?? 0;
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        if (pal[l][i]) {
          const prev = l > 0 ? dp[l-1][t-1] : 0;
          const val = prev + (i - l + 1);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            parent[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t-1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (i >= 0 && t > 0) {
    const choice = parent[i][t];
    if (!choice.skip) {
      result.unshift([choice.l, i]);
      t = choice.t;
      i = choice.l - 1;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxLength: dp[n-1][k], substrings: result };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend classic path DP problems with an additional resource constraint, evaluating proficiency in managing 3D DP states, path reconstruction via backtracking, and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) tracking exact t boosts used at position (i,j)
- Two transition options: boost or skip the current cell
- Path reconstruction by backtracking through the DP table
- Space optimization: reduce to two rows or maintain a compact parent pointer table

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, k, B) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(NEG))
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0] + B;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
          if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const boosts = [];
  let i = n - 1, j = m - 1, t = k;
  while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
    const fromTop = i > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j];
    const fromLeft = j > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j];
    const boostedFromTop = i > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;
    const boostedFromLeft = j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;

    if (boostedFromTop || boostedFromLeft) boosts.push([i, j]);
    if (boostedFromTop || (i > 0 && fromTop && !boostedFromTop)) { i--; }
    else if (j > 0) { j--; if (!boostedFromLeft && !fromLeft) t--; }
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n-1][m-1][k], boosts: boosts.reverse() };
}
```

## Complexity
- **Time:** O(n × m × k)
- **Space:** O(n × m × k) or O(m × k) with row optimization

## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests comprehensive DP design for partitioning problems with nonlinear segment costs involving both min and sum operations, requiring careful handling of base cases and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-dimensional DP over partition indices
- Range minimum query optimization within transitions
- Complete solution from base cases through reconstruction
- Space-time tradeoffs and optimization strategies

## Code Example
